  "worked_solution": "We proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we split each whole into fourths.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/42f81180846a0498783403b8bbce0523922e81ed44b40ca401e47a04ad36d157.png)\n\n \n\n- Next, we create groups of $\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/382113c4fb25a663548966d5e714bc9d1f79ba27f5de114da775ec451c8150ca.png)\n\n \n\n- Then, we count the groups.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/5e236ffc51b1faeb2f38df7fc022bd1b28a0e7dea093310941b251b24601f587.png)\n\n \n\nSo, if we divide $3$ wholes into groups of $\\frac{3}{4},$ we get $4$ groups in total.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3÷\\frac{3}{4}=\\boxed{\\text{4}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the missing number is $4.$",
